From: EglantinePS5
Subject: up: count the run-time pipeline build jobs in flight for operator new's door wait and the heap give-back (EDEN_UP_DOOR_WAIT, EDEN_UP_BUILD_GIVE_BACK), off by default

What: on a cold first start one or two builder jobs take 400-800 MiB of heap within about 2 s, twice,
at the house door, and the memory is free again about 2 s later. A refused operator new on the
file-service thread meanwhile waited 2000 ms and ended the session (v13 run1342: "EDEN_NEW_WAIT gave
up after 2000 ms", big_grew=VkPipelineBuild:320; 6 of 12 earlier door deaths). The front (headless/
main.cpp, operator new's handler) keeps such a refusal waiting while a builder job is in flight, and
(EDEN_UP_BUILD_GIVE_BACK) the heap gives its empty spaces back to the pool when the last job ends.
Both need to know when a job is in flight; this patch tells the front.

How: vk_door_job.h, a scope that calls the front's eden_door_job(1) at its start and (0) at its end
(headless/heap_arenas.inc: an atomic in-flight count, an "ended" generation and the time of the last
end). It sits beside 0592's BuildBurst::Job in the graphics and compute build lambdas, only for a
build on a VkPipelineBuilder (on_worker): the whole job, the render pass, Validate, the driver
compile and its OOM retry and the mesh compute passes, not each driver call (a mesh pipeline is up
to four calls in a row). It ends by RAII on return, on the failed-build return and on an exception,
after the build_mutex block (the waiting draw is told first). It only counts: no wait, no lock, no
log line, no allocation, nothing hashed.

Switch: on when EDEN_UP_DOOR_WAIT or EDEN_UP_BUILD_GIVE_BACK is exactly "1" and the front defines
eden_door_job (weak); read once at the first run-time build job. Off: one relaxed load of the cached
state per build job; no call. Not in the shader prep stamp: nothing built changes.

Origin and credit: our own (G1a-min of REVIEW-G1-A/B, our drafts).
---
